1
00:00:16,358 --> 00:00:34,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in regards to background and foreground mindfulness development, how should one regard the Self, so as to experience anatta, not Self?

2
00:00:39,557 --> 00:00:43,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the first thing is, one has to start seeing oneself.

3
00:00:45,848 --> 00:00:48,610
[SPEAKER_00]: As something that's present there in that experience.

4
00:00:48,730 --> 00:01:04,884
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's because people don't develop mindfulness according to the principle of simultaneous presence of the background and the foreground, as in attention and that which is not attended, but simultaneously there.

5
00:01:05,664 --> 00:01:07,866
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why they usually can't see the sense of self.

6
00:01:07,886 --> 00:01:14,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the self would be described as an elusive, ambiguous thing that you can never pin, pin it down, because you can't attend.

7
00:01:15,455 --> 00:01:18,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of self, because that sense of self, it's always in the background.

8
00:01:19,018 --> 00:01:21,020
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sense of self kind of merges with it.

9
00:01:22,201 --> 00:01:25,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Because background's always there, and sense of self, it's seemingly always there.

10
00:01:26,704 --> 00:01:28,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they share these characteristics.

11
00:01:29,887 --> 00:01:37,353
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, that results then in, in people just never seeing even, even as a phenomena, that sense of self.

12
00:01:38,046 --> 00:01:49,493
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start practicing mindfulness in terms of the discerning the peripheral phenomena without looking at them directly, you are discerning the domain where that sense of self does manifest.

13
00:01:50,574 --> 00:02:04,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you get used to recognizing it for what it is and stop trying to attend to it directly and see it as an object of your senses or something, then you get to see that it's just there, a phenomena among other phenomena.

14
00:02:05,741 --> 00:02:12,425
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when that self becomes not-self, not-myself.

15
00:02:12,445 --> 00:02:15,887
[SPEAKER_01]: You're saying it becomes... Yeah, yeah.

16
00:02:16,187 --> 00:02:18,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, that thing, that self, it stays there.

17
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:22,071
[SPEAKER_00]: But what changes is your attitude of ownership of it.

18
00:02:23,332 --> 00:02:25,313
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't see it, you automatically own it.

19
00:02:25,553 --> 00:02:26,473
[SPEAKER_00]: It is your self.

20
00:02:27,514 --> 00:02:31,156
[SPEAKER_00]: When you start seeing it, it ceases to be my self, which means ceases to be the self.

21
00:02:33,137 --> 00:02:34,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Just becomes a thing.

22
00:02:35,003 --> 00:02:38,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is a phenomenon as a background, unifying background of the experience.

23
00:02:39,247 --> 00:02:44,050
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

24
00:02:45,792 --> 00:02:52,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by seeing self, where it is, how it is... By learning how to see the self.

25
00:02:52,437 --> 00:02:53,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Learning how to see it.

26
00:02:53,678 --> 00:02:54,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't.

27
00:02:54,279 --> 00:02:55,260
[SPEAKER_00]: If you look at it, you won't see it.

28
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:57,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's behind your look.

29
00:02:59,924 --> 00:03:03,947
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why this peripheral mindfulness and vision results in knowledge.

30
00:03:05,885 --> 00:03:06,485
[SPEAKER_00]: You know it.

31
00:03:06,826 --> 00:03:09,868
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the way of seeing it, so to speak, internally.

32
00:03:10,008 --> 00:03:15,972
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't see it just by moving your attention towards it, because it's not object of your senses or your experience.

33
00:03:17,913 --> 00:03:28,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So, trying to continuously or see self, know the self, one develops then anatta.

34
00:03:29,541 --> 00:03:31,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Anatta is developed.

35
00:03:32,743 --> 00:03:34,224
[SPEAKER_00]: By properly

36
00:03:36,611 --> 00:03:43,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting to know that sense of self, eh... one will be unable to maintain the notion of ownership of it.

37
00:03:44,277 --> 00:03:46,519
[SPEAKER_00]: And that automatically is the experience of another.

38
00:03:50,943 --> 00:04:01,272
[SPEAKER_00]: When you see that the background is there to determine the foreground, inasmuch as foreground determines the background, there's absolutely no room for that self that you assume, self as the master, creator, owner of the experience.

39
00:04:06,843 --> 00:04:14,309
[SPEAKER_01]: So, normally, most people, we don't see the Self, we don't know the Self.

40
00:04:16,251 --> 00:04:25,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it sort of proliferates, expands and takes over our world, basically.

41
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:28,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, pretty much, yes, sure.

42
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:29,662
[SPEAKER_00]: The five aggregates.

43
00:04:30,363 --> 00:04:32,425
[SPEAKER_01]: This is... whatever happens, it's mine.

44
00:04:33,302 --> 00:04:41,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the assumption of I am, I am the center of this experience, I am the center of the world, basically.

45
00:04:41,404 --> 00:04:42,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

46
00:04:42,805 --> 00:04:45,145
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no end to that, to proliferation of that.

47
00:04:45,665 --> 00:04:53,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, there is an end to that, if you remove the ownership of right things, then you won't be able to maintain that gratuitous assumption anymore.

48
00:04:53,748 --> 00:04:54,948
[SPEAKER_00]: But until you do, you will.

49
00:04:55,648 --> 00:04:59,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not a matter of volition to just stop willing things to be yours.

50
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:03,831
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people sometimes resort to, like, when they're off.

51
00:05:03,951 --> 00:05:07,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Ownership, having things as mine, is a problem, because it feeds my sense of self.

52
00:05:07,771 --> 00:05:12,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, they start destroying their ownership, their property, and throwing everything, giving everything to charity, and so on.

53
00:05:12,712 --> 00:05:17,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking that by getting rid of things you own, you get rid of your sense of self.

54
00:05:17,453 --> 00:05:19,234
[SPEAKER_00]: But that, that's not how it works.

55
00:05:20,494 --> 00:05:22,894
[SPEAKER_00]: It's throwing the, throwing the baby with the bathwater.

56
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:34,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, getting rid of self-centeredness.

57
00:05:36,095 --> 00:05:40,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, which is the ownership of your sense of self.

58
00:05:40,579 --> 00:05:43,201
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to give anything up.

59
00:05:43,241 --> 00:05:45,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have to give up sensuality, your will.

60
00:05:45,422 --> 00:05:47,864
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to give up a non-virtuous behavior.

61
00:05:48,505 --> 00:05:59,473
[SPEAKER_00]: But in terms of things you own, as like items and possessions, theoretically, you don't have to give anything up, if you give up the sense of self.

62
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:01,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can't own anything.

63
00:06:01,721 --> 00:06:04,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ownership comes through the ownership of the sense of self.

64
00:06:06,282 --> 00:06:09,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why that's the... well, that's the problem, really.

65
00:06:09,924 --> 00:06:15,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to give the sense of self up, well, you need to see what that sense of self is.

66
00:06:15,466 --> 00:06:20,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just give it up, theoretically, or wish to give it up, because you don't even know what it is.

67
00:06:20,608 --> 00:06:21,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing it is giving it up.

68
00:06:22,506 --> 00:06:22,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing it.

69
00:06:23,347 --> 00:06:23,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Nope.

70
00:06:23,727 --> 00:06:24,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

71
00:06:24,267 --> 00:06:24,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

72
00:06:24,828 --> 00:06:25,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

73
00:06:25,348 --> 00:06:30,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start discerning what it is, it becomes apparent that actually it has arisen on its own.

74
00:06:31,052 --> 00:06:32,693
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impermanent in that sense.

75
00:06:33,594 --> 00:06:39,078
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not the eternal master-creator of it, or controller, which means it's automatically then given up.

76
00:06:43,741 --> 00:06:51,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing the Self was being able to know the Self in the background, correctly, and

77
00:06:58,927 --> 00:06:59,387
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be.

78
00:06:59,547 --> 00:07:00,427
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's a phenomena.

79
00:07:00,648 --> 00:07:02,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Nimitta is a phenomena as well.

80
00:07:03,189 --> 00:07:08,112
[SPEAKER_00]: But nimitta is usually a sign in regard to the established phenomena.

81
00:07:09,052 --> 00:07:14,835
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, for most people, the sense of self appears on the level of the sign.

82
00:07:14,995 --> 00:07:16,316
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't even see it as a phenomena.

83
00:07:16,856 --> 00:07:23,320
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, they could develop their mind and start discerning the sense of self, as ambiguous as it is, real as such.

84
00:07:24,118 --> 00:07:27,559
[SPEAKER_00]: An ambiguous general peripheral phenomena present here and now.

85
00:07:28,659 --> 00:07:31,340
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, they can start discerning nimitta in regard to that.

86
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:33,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Because nimitta is not a fixed thing.

87
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:38,182
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a designation for a position in a relationship.

88
00:07:40,342 --> 00:07:41,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Signs and tokens do this.

89
00:07:42,123 --> 00:07:45,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you look at them, they're gonna have their own signs and tokens.

90
00:07:46,904 --> 00:07:47,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

91
00:07:48,725 --> 00:07:49,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sign that comes

92
00:07:51,646 --> 00:08:01,733
[SPEAKER_01]: The sign that comes from knowing the Self, or seeing the Self phenomenon, that sign is anatta.

93
00:08:02,333 --> 00:08:02,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Anatta.

94
00:08:04,454 --> 00:08:05,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not automatically, though.

95
00:08:06,456 --> 00:08:09,318
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were told to look at it like that, then you will see it.

96
00:08:10,198 --> 00:08:12,560
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the external instruction is necessary.

97
00:08:14,381 --> 00:08:19,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if Pradyojana could become very authentic in this sense of Self and see it like a like a relatively

98
00:08:20,955 --> 00:08:21,915
[SPEAKER_00]: dependent nature.

99
00:08:22,315 --> 00:08:28,037
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, he will still remain assuming the ownership of it.

100
00:08:28,977 --> 00:08:48,402
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you just pointed out to actually see even that as any other phenomena, then you will start discerning the phenomena of the nimitta, as in the signs and tokens in regard to that phenomenon of self as actually not in my control, not mine, not pleasurable, dependent, thus not the self.

101
00:08:49,797 --> 00:08:51,778
[SPEAKER_01]: These signs, those are like meanings.

102
00:08:51,818 --> 00:08:52,219
Meanings?

103
00:08:52,419 --> 00:08:54,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course, yeah, yeah, yeah.

104
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:55,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the meaning of self?

105
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:56,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, signs and token, yeah.

106
00:08:56,501 --> 00:08:58,622
[SPEAKER_00]: If you didn't know it... That's what the sign is.

107
00:08:58,682 --> 00:08:59,643
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a point in meaning.

108
00:08:59,943 --> 00:09:00,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Points at something.

109
00:09:01,344 --> 00:09:02,265
[SPEAKER_00]: A meaning of something.

110
00:09:02,525 --> 00:09:02,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

111
00:09:03,625 --> 00:09:04,006
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

112
00:09:04,026 --> 00:09:06,487
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a, like a, a reference relationship.

113
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:17,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would be correct to say that the sign of the foreground

114
00:09:18,615 --> 00:09:19,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

115
00:09:23,258 --> 00:09:24,579
[SPEAKER_00]: The sign is always in the background.

116
00:09:24,839 --> 00:09:27,741
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by looking at the background, you're going to start discerning the signs.

117
00:09:28,041 --> 00:09:30,282
[SPEAKER_00]: By discerning the signs, you're becoming aware of the background.

118
00:09:31,042 --> 00:09:43,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by seeing the foreground, not going into it too much, not attending to the particulars too much.

119
00:09:44,989 --> 00:09:45,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, too much, in a sense.

120
00:09:46,450 --> 00:09:51,634
[SPEAKER_00]: You can attend to them if it won't compromise your mindfulness of the experience as a whole.

121
00:09:54,355 --> 00:10:02,361
[SPEAKER_00]: But if attending to the particulars of the foreground takes your mind away from the mindfulness of the experience as a whole, well, then you're compromising it.

122
00:10:05,323 --> 00:10:11,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Because being able to see the sign of the foreground, the background...

123
00:10:12,667 --> 00:10:15,969
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be an established mindfulness.

124
00:10:17,410 --> 00:10:17,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

125
00:10:18,191 --> 00:10:19,291
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be right mindfulness.

126
00:10:19,932 --> 00:10:20,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, exactly.

127
00:10:20,832 --> 00:10:22,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that be the middle way?

128
00:10:23,094 --> 00:10:24,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is the middle way, yeah.

129
00:10:25,235 --> 00:10:26,176
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the beginning of it.

130
00:10:26,756 --> 00:10:26,896
Yeah.

131
00:10:27,376 --> 00:10:34,821
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stick to that kind of mindfulness, you will develop, then, the discernment of the sign of, even further, which is the sign of your mind.

132
00:10:35,882 --> 00:10:38,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the right view after that, as the Buddha said.

133
00:10:39,644 --> 00:10:40,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, everything else.

134
00:10:42,277 --> 00:10:44,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the middle way, exactly because of that.

135
00:10:44,599 --> 00:10:50,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're neither giving in to the senses of the mind, nor you're mystifying and dismissing the whole thing going the other direction.

136
00:10:51,025 --> 00:10:52,125
[SPEAKER_00]: You're kind of in between.

137
00:10:54,327 --> 00:10:55,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Like with this, this is.

138
00:10:55,749 --> 00:10:56,129
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

139
00:10:57,310 --> 00:11:01,273
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not from me here to there, or from there to me here.

140
00:11:01,534 --> 00:11:04,516
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just these two things arise and cease simultaneously together.

141
00:11:05,457 --> 00:11:07,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Like nāmarūpa and viññāṇa, viññāṇa and nāmarūpa.

142
00:11:07,739 --> 00:11:08,099
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

143
00:11:08,139 --> 00:11:08,940
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all that's there.

144
00:11:10,143 --> 00:11:16,507
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not fully understanding that, you assume yourself in it, as the owner of it.

145
00:11:17,587 --> 00:11:23,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if one is in that state, state, I don't know how to use that.

146
00:11:24,011 --> 00:11:26,232
[SPEAKER_00]: But go on, ask a question, then we can reformulate it.

147
00:11:26,272 --> 00:11:33,396
[SPEAKER_01]: If one is doing right mindfulness, balancing in the middle way, then when you act,

148
00:11:40,910 --> 00:11:43,792
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't act too... Too unwholesomely.

149
00:11:43,832 --> 00:11:44,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Too unwholesomely.

150
00:11:44,252 --> 00:11:44,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

151
00:11:44,712 --> 00:11:46,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the right effort.

152
00:11:46,153 --> 00:11:46,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

153
00:11:46,614 --> 00:11:57,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Because greed, aversion, delusion is... it counters the balance, even in slightest amounts.

154
00:11:58,942 --> 00:12:08,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So somebody who is committed, discerning the practice of that balancing, as you said, then will just see that it's completely incompatible with it.

155
00:12:10,033 --> 00:12:13,455
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't practice the dhamma and engage in greed, aversion, delusion.

156
00:12:13,935 --> 00:12:22,060
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do, you fall from dhamma for that period of time, whatever you're engaging with, for however long you choose to.

157
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:25,862
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, you can you can call it balancing in a way.

158
00:12:26,522 --> 00:12:29,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Think of it like balancing you get used to.

159
00:12:29,644 --> 00:12:31,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you balance something for the sake of balancing.

160
00:12:32,706 --> 00:12:36,768
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you, within the balance, you start discerning further opportunity for balance.

161
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:45,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Because somebody who has never ever stepped back and become aware of their own situation, doing that first time, would feel like, wow, that's it.

162
00:12:45,604 --> 00:12:46,904
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the experience as a whole.

163
00:12:47,645 --> 00:12:52,367
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you get used to that, then you see, okay, well, now you can actually take the same principle even further.

164
00:12:53,207 --> 00:13:01,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there is... there are subtler, more peripheral backgrounds to regard to your foreground, and there are more particular foregrounds with their own limiters and so on.

165
00:13:01,831 --> 00:13:02,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same principle,

166
00:13:03,513 --> 00:13:04,914
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you can take it further and further.

167
00:13:05,154 --> 00:13:06,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And how far you take it?

168
00:13:06,135 --> 00:13:08,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, as long as it takes until you're free from suffering.

169
00:13:08,897 --> 00:13:10,719
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your measure.

170
00:13:10,799 --> 00:13:12,480
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what nibbāna is.

171
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:22,308
[SPEAKER_00]: When that perfect balance of non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion becomes like fully cemented form of being here and now.

172
00:13:22,869 --> 00:13:23,909
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no other way.

173
00:13:24,830 --> 00:13:33,117
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why when he studies himself in nibbāna, and the one who doesn't develop it fully, he falls from nibbāna, as the Buddha would describe in the suttas.

174
00:13:34,462 --> 00:13:40,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a great sort of, you know, I don't know, spiritual wave of energy that sweeps everything and just...

175
00:13:41,745 --> 00:13:43,946
[SPEAKER_00]: It's diligence and repetition and hard work.

176
00:13:44,226 --> 00:13:53,569
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would always say, whatever was to be obtained by the manly effort, manly strength, manly commitment or whatever it was exactly he used, I have obtained.

177
00:13:55,130 --> 00:13:59,451
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't given to him by by luck or mystical experience or something.

178
00:13:59,491 --> 00:13:59,671
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

179
00:14:01,392 --> 00:14:01,912
[SPEAKER_00]: He saw it.

180
00:14:02,072 --> 00:14:02,732
[SPEAKER_00]: He understood it.

181
00:14:02,792 --> 00:14:03,233
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd done it.

182
00:14:03,633 --> 00:14:04,193
[SPEAKER_00]: He developed it.

183
00:14:12,681 --> 00:14:15,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Balance, maybe a better word is composure.

184
00:14:16,202 --> 00:14:17,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, composure.

185
00:14:18,703 --> 00:14:25,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Balance, kind of, again, I mean, it's not, it's not a wrong term, but it's going to be misunderstood, no doubt about it.

186
00:14:25,646 --> 00:14:37,971
[SPEAKER_00]: But you think about balance, think about composure, think about maintaining the sight of the background while not ignoring the foreground, or doing the... being engaged, attending to the foreground without losing the sight of the background.

187
00:14:38,705 --> 00:14:42,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's a form of balance that you... the more you do, the better you get at it.

188
00:14:43,246 --> 00:14:43,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Jhāna is the same.

189
00:14:44,026 --> 00:14:44,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle.

190
00:14:46,986 --> 00:14:51,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Establishing your mind outside the domain of sensuality until it gets steadied in it.

191
00:14:52,227 --> 00:14:56,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sīla... sīla is the emulation of that balance.

192
00:14:56,768 --> 00:15:00,889
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't understand it, you don't know it, but you start emulating it.

193
00:15:01,109 --> 00:15:01,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

194
00:15:01,769 --> 00:15:06,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Which can then give rise to the actual understanding, because you have the setup in front of you.

195
00:15:06,210 --> 00:15:07,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still not recognizing it,

196
00:15:08,070 --> 00:15:11,833
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're emulating that, that can then, eventually, bring the recognition.

197
00:15:12,294 --> 00:15:16,037
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't recognize something, if there is... if that thing is not in front of you.

198
00:15:16,077 --> 00:15:19,941
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you recognize it as such and such, if it's not there altogether?

199
00:15:20,962 --> 00:15:24,925
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sīla and the virtue being the prerequisite to any practice of dharma, it's exactly that.

200
00:15:25,546 --> 00:15:28,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Emulating the right order, emulating the correct balance.

201
00:15:30,370 --> 00:15:33,193
[SPEAKER_00]: The non-assumption, non-greed, non-aversion, non-self-centeredness.

202
00:15:33,897 --> 00:15:45,482
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, that's why sīla in itself won't result in the dhamma, because you can emulate all you want, if you don't make the effort to understand and succeed in understanding what you're emulating, then you're not... then you haven't developed it.

203
00:15:46,843 --> 00:15:52,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's almost like training wheels, maybe, like keeping the rules with like training wheels.

204
00:15:52,525 --> 00:15:54,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, yeah, could be, yeah.

205
00:15:55,466 --> 00:15:57,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you can still be riding, you don't have to be...

206
00:15:57,487 --> 00:16:01,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like you... once you've understood

207
00:16:02,293 --> 00:16:06,456
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma, the principle, you got the middle way, you're balancing there.

208
00:16:06,996 --> 00:16:09,138
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't put... you let go of virtue.

209
00:16:09,298 --> 00:16:14,041
[SPEAKER_00]: No, exactly, because the purpose of the virtue is now your purpose of the dhamma.

210
00:16:15,282 --> 00:16:18,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the purpose of the training wheels was to keep you straight and flowing.

211
00:16:19,985 --> 00:16:22,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you're keeping straight and flowing without the training wheels.

212
00:16:23,347 --> 00:16:23,627
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

213
00:16:23,647 --> 00:16:26,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But yet, you've gone... you are straight and flowing.

214
00:16:26,429 --> 00:16:27,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

215
00:16:28,230 --> 00:16:29,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've gone beyond.

216
00:16:29,922 --> 00:16:34,823
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say, in a way, you go beyond, deep, beyond the precepts.

217
00:16:34,843 --> 00:16:35,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

218
00:16:37,104 --> 00:16:38,804
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there were no precepts in the beginning.

219
00:16:39,265 --> 00:16:43,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the Buddha would teach dhamma and that's exactly what people would understand.

220
00:16:43,366 --> 00:16:46,307
[SPEAKER_00]: There wouldn't be many misunderstandings and wrong views about it.

221
00:16:47,027 --> 00:16:49,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those who understood it, they understood it right to the core.

222
00:16:49,467 --> 00:16:53,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, immediately, there was a reference point to anything that shouldn't be done from that point of view.

223
00:16:54,269 --> 00:16:58,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything that would be countering that balance or affecting it.

224
00:17:00,525 --> 00:17:10,473
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as the clarity that Balan started waning away, basically, it was sort of almost to the same extent, it was the increase in bad behavior.

225
00:17:13,115 --> 00:17:24,083
[SPEAKER_00]: But initially, like, there were no rules about, I don't know, eating, like, yeah, yeah, like not eating in the afternoon, for example, or eating once a day.

226
00:17:24,423 --> 00:17:26,385
[SPEAKER_00]: They would eat, they would go pindapad at night.

227
00:17:27,677 --> 00:17:31,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, some of the rules are risen in response to, like, interaction with society.

228
00:17:31,539 --> 00:17:34,801
[SPEAKER_00]: It had nothing to do with dhamma or with obstructing the balance and so on.

229
00:17:36,202 --> 00:17:41,845
[SPEAKER_00]: But those that did arise on account of monks doing unwholesome things, that all came later.

230
00:17:42,125 --> 00:17:43,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Because initially, it was very clear.

231
00:17:45,767 --> 00:17:48,388
[SPEAKER_00]: What is an obstacle to the dhamma?

232
00:17:50,029 --> 00:17:51,810
[SPEAKER_00]: And what isn't?

233
00:17:51,890 --> 00:17:54,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something that isn't an obstacle can be a rule as well.

234
00:17:54,752 --> 00:17:56,593
[SPEAKER_00]: We have many of the rules that are not actually obstacles.

235
00:17:58,406 --> 00:18:06,851
[SPEAKER_00]: To the dhamma, it kind of arose and formulated in response to, like, as I said, interaction with people and societies and kings and whatnot.

236
00:18:07,992 --> 00:18:12,375
[SPEAKER_00]: But the ones that they were obstacle, yeah, they stayed obstacle.

237
00:18:13,395 --> 00:18:14,056
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't...

238
00:18:14,096 --> 00:18:20,980
[SPEAKER_00]: If something is an obstacle to the dhamma, it doesn't depend on the society and the social context that dhamma is practicing.

239
00:18:21,140 --> 00:18:27,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It always relate to this universal principles of greed, aversion, delusion, being, obstruction to that balance, to that composure.

240
00:18:30,342 --> 00:18:31,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's when people try to argue.

241
00:18:32,483 --> 00:18:38,866
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I heard something as silly as that, that today you don't have to be celibate in order to practice dhamma.

242
00:18:38,886 --> 00:18:47,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Back in the day, they needed to do that, because it was kind of dependent on the social context and everything else, so it's just plain ridiculous.

243
00:18:47,289 --> 00:18:54,892
[SPEAKER_01]: If anything, today you need to be twice as hard celibate than back then, because your sensuality is everywhere, completely accessible.

244
00:18:55,993 --> 00:18:58,834
[SPEAKER_00]: And the minds are more proliferated and weaker than back then.

245
00:18:59,650 --> 00:19:02,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people don't deal much with hardship today anyway.

246
00:19:02,952 --> 00:19:03,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Very few do.

247
00:19:04,393 --> 00:19:06,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're not naturally tough.

248
00:19:06,894 --> 00:19:10,876
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, if anything, today you need to toughen up even more than back in the day.

249
00:19:12,477 --> 00:19:17,860
[SPEAKER_00]: But if something is a direct obstacle, it's a universal obstacle, because dhamma is universal.

250
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:21,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't dismiss it because of a social context or requirement.

251
00:19:22,023 --> 00:19:26,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, eating in the afternoon, wearing this type of robes or doing that, that's all, yeah, sure.

252
00:19:26,902 --> 00:19:28,083
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing to do with the dhamma.

253
00:19:28,163 --> 00:19:30,644
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a way of expression, a way of relating to other people.

254
00:19:31,325 --> 00:19:33,146
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a problem for that very reason either.

255
00:19:37,268 --> 00:19:47,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you have that basic, or that understanding of balancing, or the middle way... Or practice of mindfulness and composure.

256
00:19:47,855 --> 00:19:49,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

257
00:19:52,558 --> 00:19:55,940
[SPEAKER_01]: You were saying about diligence, you know, just...

258
00:19:57,656 --> 00:20:01,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Not giving in to unwholesome.

259
00:20:02,339 --> 00:20:04,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, well, we spoke the other day, yes.

260
00:20:04,561 --> 00:20:05,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, yeah.

261
00:20:06,222 --> 00:20:20,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the thing, you... By practicing mindfulness correctly, by being properly composed, the issues of greed, aversion, delusion become clearer.

262
00:20:23,175 --> 00:20:27,278
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you know exactly the extent of your responsibility in those things.

263
00:20:27,983 --> 00:20:35,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means then you know exactly, or gradually, you increase the accuracy of your recognition of what you need to do.

264
00:20:37,150 --> 00:20:43,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're not saying no to everything and everyone all the time, because that's what restraint is all about.

265
00:20:43,355 --> 00:20:43,695
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

266
00:20:44,035 --> 00:20:46,137
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about restraining greed, aversion, delusion.

267
00:20:47,178 --> 00:20:50,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Not tolerate greed, aversion, delusion, not detain you, not indulge you.

268
00:20:50,921 --> 00:20:52,782
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it isn't that, it's not your problem.

269
00:20:55,237 --> 00:20:58,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's that's how you become aware of exactly what you need to do.

270
00:20:58,578 --> 00:20:59,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Your work.

271
00:20:59,899 --> 00:21:03,040
[SPEAKER_00]: And by being aware of your work, then you can be diligent in regard to that work.

272
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:05,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what it means.

273
00:21:06,161 --> 00:21:10,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, doing it whenever it's necessary.

274
00:21:11,283 --> 00:21:22,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a chore, but chore that can arise at any given time, or not arise for quite a while, but whenever it arises, it is done.

275
00:21:22,706 --> 00:21:24,107
[SPEAKER_00]: There is absolutely no

276
00:21:25,220 --> 00:21:27,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, I'll do it a bit later.

277
00:21:27,441 --> 00:21:28,042
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll do it tomorrow.

278
00:21:28,062 --> 00:21:28,742
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't be bothered now.

279
00:21:28,762 --> 00:21:30,804
[SPEAKER_01]: My meditation only starts at seven tonight.

280
00:21:30,924 --> 00:21:31,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

281
00:21:31,264 --> 00:21:32,445
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll deal with my sensuality.

282
00:21:32,505 --> 00:21:34,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And you certainly cannot put a time schedule on it.

283
00:21:35,166 --> 00:21:37,248
[SPEAKER_01]: One hour of dealing with sensuality.

284
00:21:37,268 --> 00:21:37,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

285
00:21:37,708 --> 00:21:38,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

286
00:21:39,609 --> 00:21:43,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I can go back and sort of forget about it, because I've done my work for today.

287
00:21:43,412 --> 00:21:43,532
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

288
00:21:43,952 --> 00:21:45,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes the whole day will be a chore.

289
00:21:46,974 --> 00:21:52,538
[SPEAKER_00]: But on some other days, you're going to have, like, weeks, months of no chores.

290
00:21:53,638 --> 00:21:54,739
[SPEAKER_00]: And then arahant, obviously.

291
00:21:55,893 --> 00:21:57,714
[SPEAKER_00]: He has done his duty, as the Buddha would say.

292
00:21:57,794 --> 00:21:58,994
[SPEAKER_00]: He finished all his chores.

293
00:22:00,355 --> 00:22:02,055
[SPEAKER_00]: He has laid down the burden of duty.

294
00:22:02,595 --> 00:22:03,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he's done.

295
00:22:03,376 --> 00:22:03,776
[SPEAKER_00]: He's done.

296
00:22:04,976 --> 00:22:07,177
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't need to do anything else.

297
00:22:07,197 --> 00:22:09,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, as a monk, that's pretty much the only thing you need to do.

298
00:22:12,379 --> 00:22:19,602
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, given the obviously practical necessities of patching your robes, walking Pindapad and so on.

299
00:22:19,882 --> 00:22:21,663
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't take your whole day.

300
00:22:21,703 --> 00:22:25,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the rest of the time, that is, that should be the monk's priority.

301
00:22:26,767 --> 00:22:28,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Not tolerating greed, aversion, delusion.

302
00:22:28,207 --> 00:22:32,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarifying the work in regard to greed, aversion, delusion, and then being diligent in regard to that.

303
00:22:34,009 --> 00:22:40,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if sometimes you wake up at two o'clock at night, and you wake up with a mind with, I don't know, affected by a hindrance or something, you have to do the work.

304
00:22:40,751 --> 00:22:42,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't go back to sleep until you do the work.

305
00:22:43,232 --> 00:22:52,675
[SPEAKER_00]: But if sometimes, if you're sleepy at ten o'clock in the morning, and not as a hindrance, just as a genuine tiredness, because you got up at two o'clock at night, then you can go to sleep,

306
00:22:56,967 --> 00:22:59,169
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't want to be tolerating greed, aversion, delusion.

307
00:23:00,910 --> 00:23:03,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where the work is.

308
00:23:03,112 --> 00:23:12,859
[SPEAKER_00]: And ultimately, of course, for anything sensed, like any sensed desire related, sensuality is the chief of the desires.

309
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:15,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality, sexuality.

310
00:23:15,761 --> 00:23:20,684
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't tolerate that, you won't be tolerating anything inferior to that.

311
00:23:21,665 --> 00:23:26,669
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, if you free yourself from sexuality, you won't be attached to any other sensual experience.

312
00:23:32,022 --> 00:23:34,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, we go back to the importance of celibacy.

313
00:23:34,824 --> 00:23:36,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

314
00:23:36,625 --> 00:23:42,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you also have to see the importance of... you've got to know that it's important to do that work.

315
00:23:42,828 --> 00:23:43,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, of course.

316
00:23:43,889 --> 00:23:44,409
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's what I mean.

317
00:23:44,970 --> 00:23:46,250
[SPEAKER_00]: By... that's what Buddha said.

318
00:23:46,851 --> 00:23:54,095
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands... he understands sensuality, the gratification of it, then the danger in it.

319
00:23:54,735 --> 00:23:59,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you understand the danger of slacking that particular work.

320
00:23:59,697 --> 00:24:02,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You understand the danger of not doing that chore.

321
00:24:03,319 --> 00:24:04,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the danger?

322
00:24:05,821 --> 00:24:13,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Any possible misfortune that can come your way is a result of you being in the domain where misfortunes can occur.

323
00:24:15,927 --> 00:24:24,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And you place yourself in that domain by not disavowing, by not saying no to sensuality.

324
00:24:24,533 --> 00:24:26,134
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, not freeing yourself from it.

325
00:24:26,314 --> 00:24:28,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, people can say no, but still want it.

326
00:24:30,284 --> 00:24:32,385
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to stop wanting it.

327
00:24:34,386 --> 00:24:41,029
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the restraint is necessary, of course, because you can't, oh, I don't want it, but I'm engaging with it in my behavior.

328
00:24:41,430 --> 00:24:44,971
[SPEAKER_00]: No, your behavior perpetuates your wanting of it.

329
00:24:46,452 --> 00:24:52,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to stop the sensual behavior, you want to practice restraint, but that in itself is not enough either.

330
00:24:52,975 --> 00:24:58,638
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to use that, then, to actually discern and recognize that this is the extent of sensual pleasure.

331
00:25:00,585 --> 00:25:01,365
[SPEAKER_00]: No more, no less.

332
00:25:01,865 --> 00:25:05,187
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the extent of it, and I do not want it.

333
00:25:08,087 --> 00:25:20,191
[SPEAKER_00]: But until you discern the extent, you can't then decide, or rather you can't then develop your repulsion towards it, because you don't even know how big it is.

334
00:25:20,512 --> 00:25:21,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're like

335
00:25:22,135 --> 00:25:25,696
[SPEAKER_00]: You say, you know, this sensuality usually then ends up being aimed to this particular thing, that particular thing.

336
00:25:25,996 --> 00:25:35,699
[SPEAKER_00]: But overall, your mind still finds the idea, the phenomenon, the most general phenomenon of sensuality, finds it pleasing, agreeable, welcomes it.

337
00:25:37,060 --> 00:25:37,660
[SPEAKER_00]: On that level.

338
00:25:37,860 --> 00:25:38,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

339
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:47,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not engage in anything by body, by speech, not even necessarily in coarse thoughts, but you still have the attitude of, oh, this is... this is nice.

340
00:25:48,271 --> 00:25:52,014
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you wouldn't call nice something, if that something is dangerous.

341
00:25:53,455 --> 00:25:57,118
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter how nicely coated it is, you would say, oh, this is dangerous.

342
00:25:57,218 --> 00:26:00,881
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he understands the gratification in it.

343
00:26:02,342 --> 00:26:05,445
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands the danger, and thus he knows the escape.

344
00:26:06,385 --> 00:26:12,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't understand the gratification, well, you can't understand the danger, because the danger can apply only on that level.

345
00:26:13,030 --> 00:26:14,732
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger as in, I don't want this.

346
00:26:19,826 --> 00:26:23,667
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of that mindfulness, you will slip from that mindfulness.

347
00:26:25,028 --> 00:26:32,070
[SPEAKER_01]: You will slip from the composure that you understood or developed by going in to the senses.

348
00:26:33,090 --> 00:26:33,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

349
00:26:33,450 --> 00:26:34,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the only way to slip.

350
00:26:35,251 --> 00:26:36,951
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another thing that will become apparent.

351
00:26:37,471 --> 00:26:40,132
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't slip by not making enough effort.

352
00:26:41,212 --> 00:26:43,493
[SPEAKER_00]: You slip by giving in to the opposing effort.

353
00:26:44,569 --> 00:26:50,290
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why all you need to do is just watch the gate, so to speak, the gatekeeper, as the Buddha would give those similes.

354
00:26:50,850 --> 00:26:53,811
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to chase everything in or run after everything that goes out.

355
00:26:53,871 --> 00:26:58,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stay at the gate, because you know that's where the problems arise, if they arise.

356
00:26:58,492 --> 00:26:59,872
[SPEAKER_00]: The door, yeah, the sense doors.

357
00:27:00,192 --> 00:27:05,653
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't give in to anything, just on that little level, no problems, nothing can actually arise.

358
00:27:05,953 --> 00:27:10,955
[SPEAKER_00]: But giving in and then actually making that effort of giving in, yeah, that's how you fall for mindfulness.

359
00:27:11,535 --> 00:27:13,035
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness becomes effortless.

360
00:27:14,151 --> 00:27:15,552
[SPEAKER_00]: When your mind is above sensuality.

361
00:27:19,113 --> 00:27:33,798
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as a reflection, you can... you can look at perception in the foreground and... Oh, that's... that's... that's the extent of sensuality.

362
00:27:34,338 --> 00:27:34,658
[SPEAKER_00]: If you...

363
00:27:35,278 --> 00:27:38,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception in the foreground and feeling in the background.

364
00:27:38,999 --> 00:27:40,240
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I was talking about in the essay.

365
00:27:41,556 --> 00:27:46,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling, feeling and perceiving with perception.

366
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:49,322
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly the point.

367
00:27:49,362 --> 00:27:52,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are after pleasure in regard to agreeable perceptions.

368
00:27:53,386 --> 00:27:56,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means the feeling in the background motivates you.

369
00:27:58,690 --> 00:28:00,011
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the important bit here.

370
00:28:00,472 --> 00:28:05,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're just looking at perceptions, you'll be avoiding, you'll be denying, you'll be trying to get rid of it.

371
00:28:05,596 --> 00:28:06,917
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not uprooting anything.

372
00:28:10,460 --> 00:28:13,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And in a way, that's how you realize, that's how you can discern the gratification.

373
00:28:15,501 --> 00:28:22,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you're restrained, you don't go and try to deny perceptions, in terms of you have a central thought arises.

374
00:28:23,944 --> 00:28:25,044
[SPEAKER_00]: That has happened on its own.

375
00:28:25,224 --> 00:28:26,045
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not your problem.

376
00:28:27,465 --> 00:28:29,906
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is if you give in to it, if you choose to act out of it.

377
00:28:31,186 --> 00:28:32,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's that's obvious problem.

378
00:28:33,027 --> 00:28:36,168
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the problem is also if you if you try to get rid of those thoughts as well.

379
00:28:36,468 --> 00:28:37,708
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop thinking, I must stop thinking.

380
00:28:39,356 --> 00:28:54,869
[SPEAKER_00]: And people do that very often, because it's actually unpleasant to have those thoughts enduring about in your mind, because you start to feel it more, the pressure of it, the pull, the pain, the dart, as the Buddha would call it.

381
00:28:56,130 --> 00:28:59,492
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't feel it, you'll never realize the extent of gratification of it.

382
00:29:01,274 --> 00:29:06,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you shouldn't definitely not give in by body, speech or mind, but you should certainly not try to get rid of it either.

383
00:29:06,939 --> 00:29:08,620
[SPEAKER_00]: You should be mindful in regard to it.

384
00:29:10,334 --> 00:29:16,658
[SPEAKER_00]: And allow it to endure, just so that you can see the extent of the prospect of pleasure implied in it.

385
00:29:18,539 --> 00:29:20,941
[SPEAKER_00]: When you do, then you see the extent of the danger.

386
00:29:22,622 --> 00:29:25,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can say, I don't want it, on that level.

387
00:29:25,584 --> 00:29:26,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it will work.

388
00:29:27,205 --> 00:29:33,689
[SPEAKER_00]: If you say, I don't want it, to a too particular level, you're not applying that attitude of not wanting it.

389
00:29:33,729 --> 00:29:34,730
[SPEAKER_00]: This is unwanted.

390
00:29:35,270 --> 00:29:42,114
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not applying it on a level of the nature of sensuality, which is far more general than the particular manifestation of it.

391
00:29:44,315 --> 00:29:46,476
[SPEAKER_00]: And that generality is where you feel it.

392
00:29:46,937 --> 00:29:55,661
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sensuality, feeling in regards to sensuality, motivates your efforts, craving for that pleasure.

393
00:29:57,202 --> 00:30:03,386
[SPEAKER_01]: What about seeing, knowing feeling, seeing perception?

394
00:30:06,456 --> 00:30:10,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see that difference?

395
00:30:11,980 --> 00:30:14,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you know the feeling when you stop craving in regard to it.

396
00:30:15,423 --> 00:30:18,125
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know the feeling when you psychologize or rationalize it.

397
00:30:19,666 --> 00:30:21,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you're just resorting to your perceptions again.

398
00:30:21,968 --> 00:30:32,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you are... if you see perception or no perceptions correctly, then why would you give in to them?

399
00:30:34,557 --> 00:30:37,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your feeling is still not free from craving.

400
00:30:38,301 --> 00:30:44,988
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know the feeling as well... Well, you don't really know the perceptions until you know the feeling.

401
00:30:45,268 --> 00:30:47,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't know the feeling until you know the perceptions.

402
00:30:47,250 --> 00:30:50,413
[SPEAKER_01]: And you won't be giving in to perception because... Yeah.

403
00:30:50,473 --> 00:30:54,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you're not giving in to perceptions because of perceptions.

404
00:30:54,197 --> 00:30:56,980
[SPEAKER_00]: People give in to perceptions because of the craving in regard to feeling.

405
00:30:57,823 --> 00:31:01,526
[SPEAKER_00]: And they themselves create that magical connection from feeling into perceptions.

406
00:31:01,646 --> 00:31:04,169
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what I... this is what will satisfy.

407
00:31:04,229 --> 00:31:05,910
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's always on the domain of perception.

408
00:31:06,370 --> 00:31:08,172
[SPEAKER_00]: And feeling always remains on the domain of feeling.

409
00:31:08,672 --> 00:31:16,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Just recognizing that independence of each other in itself can significantly approve the craving from feeling towards my perceptions.

410
00:31:16,499 --> 00:31:17,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's no point.

411
00:31:17,820 --> 00:31:18,401
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no connection.

412
00:31:18,421 --> 00:31:19,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable.

413
00:31:20,655 --> 00:31:30,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing that they are just completely invisible to each other, so to speak, these two domains, you keep assuming this directionality, so to speak, of my dissatisfaction.

414
00:31:30,361 --> 00:31:32,402
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people say our sensuality can never satisfy.

415
00:31:32,462 --> 00:31:36,405
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't, because it's applied here, and the problem was over here.

416
00:31:36,585 --> 00:31:37,845
[SPEAKER_00]: You want that there.

417
00:31:38,246 --> 00:31:43,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of the problem here, I magically believe that me doing this here, gets rid of that.

418
00:31:43,349 --> 00:31:43,729
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't.

419
00:31:43,989 --> 00:31:45,290
[SPEAKER_00]: It just distracts you from it.

420
00:31:45,943 --> 00:31:54,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you forgetting about the problem of craving in regard to your feeling, you may... you develop a belief, which is why sensuality is an assumption.

421
00:31:54,631 --> 00:31:55,351
[SPEAKER_00]: It's upādāna.

422
00:31:56,873 --> 00:31:58,835
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop a belief that, okay, that's... that's it.

423
00:31:58,915 --> 00:31:59,635
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what this is.

424
00:32:00,196 --> 00:32:01,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely magical connection between the two.

425
00:32:01,977 --> 00:32:10,325
[SPEAKER_00]: We cannot... We try to manage our perceptions to get what feelings we want.

426
00:32:12,107 --> 00:32:12,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, sure, sure.

427
00:32:13,047 --> 00:32:15,728
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's just... that is the way it has to be.

428
00:32:16,188 --> 00:32:19,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even when you start practicing restraint, it's a form of managing your perceptions.

429
00:32:20,370 --> 00:32:21,990
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't do it for its own sake.

430
00:32:23,171 --> 00:32:27,212
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it for the sake of restraint, so that then you can develop the understanding of it.

431
00:32:28,512 --> 00:32:32,654
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just doing it for the sake of doing it, just management, then that's all it's going to be.

432
00:32:32,834 --> 00:32:33,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Management.

433
00:32:34,314 --> 00:32:37,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be restrained your whole life, but not understand anything.

434
00:32:37,415 --> 00:32:39,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Still better than being non-restrained.

435
00:32:40,010 --> 00:32:43,273
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not the dhamma, not the practice of it, certainly not the understanding of it.

436
00:32:44,474 --> 00:32:46,316
[SPEAKER_01]: I think you can interfere with feelings.

437
00:32:47,677 --> 00:32:49,018
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem.

438
00:32:49,258 --> 00:32:53,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think you can go into that feeling and control it, manage it?

439
00:32:54,803 --> 00:33:01,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you think you own the feelings, thus you feel entitled to... That's why the Buddha would say, well, which feeling do you own then?

440
00:33:02,409 --> 00:33:04,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it a pleasant feeling, unpleasant or neutral?

441
00:33:05,572 --> 00:33:07,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you were truly the owner of

442
00:33:08,202 --> 00:33:12,404
[SPEAKER_00]: The feeling domain, you would only feel pleasure all the time.

443
00:33:14,705 --> 00:33:17,086
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact is that they come on their own and go their own.

444
00:33:17,106 --> 00:33:19,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which one are you the master of?

445
00:33:20,667 --> 00:33:22,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what can you do with feelings?

446
00:33:22,528 --> 00:33:25,950
[SPEAKER_01]: What can you do with feelings?

447
00:33:26,110 --> 00:33:28,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Allow them to endure, not act out of them.

448
00:33:29,992 --> 00:33:33,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will automatically result in mindfulness of them.

449
00:33:36,755 --> 00:33:54,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means being able to accept that initial pain of not acting out of feelings, wanting more, more of this, wanting less of that, more of this pleasure, less of that pain, wanting distraction in regard to this oppressing boredom, anxiety.

450
00:33:55,639 --> 00:33:56,599
[SPEAKER_00]: All those things are felt.

451
00:33:58,539 --> 00:34:06,281
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's where the main problem is rooted in regard to those phenomena, which means you just have to learn how to endure them and not act out of them.

452
00:34:07,680 --> 00:34:08,520
[SPEAKER_00]: But allow them to be.

453
00:34:09,461 --> 00:34:18,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only problem is you not being able to help yourself, but assume the ownership of them.

454
00:34:19,905 --> 00:34:24,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to undo that type of helplessness.

455
00:34:25,708 --> 00:34:34,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to get to understand them, so that then conceiving that you're their owner becomes inconceivable.

456
00:34:39,665 --> 00:34:40,906
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't really interfere.

457
00:34:41,847 --> 00:34:43,969
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't interfere with any of the aggregates, for that matter.

458
00:34:44,569 --> 00:34:48,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Even your intentions, which are usually the closest to the self point of view.

459
00:34:49,914 --> 00:34:55,859
[SPEAKER_00]: The only... the only things you can intend, are things that are given as options, as possibilities.

460
00:34:57,720 --> 00:35:04,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, even if you intend something, right here, right now, in this immediacy, it's because it was an option to do so.

461
00:35:04,306 --> 00:35:08,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And those options were already arisen, given on that peripheral level, on their own.

462
00:35:13,493 --> 00:35:17,554
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even think of... you can't think anything from pure nothingness into being.

463
00:35:17,614 --> 00:35:18,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't create that.

464
00:35:18,814 --> 00:35:19,575
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not on you.

465
00:35:20,855 --> 00:35:22,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Things are given on the peripheral level.

466
00:35:22,715 --> 00:35:28,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, there are actual things present, as in the ones that you attend, on account of other things you don't attend to.

467
00:35:29,077 --> 00:35:32,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, for example, I'm just having this in my mind now.

468
00:35:32,378 --> 00:35:33,698
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like art.

469
00:35:35,399 --> 00:35:40,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I draw a picture that I've just created by myself.

470
00:35:40,780 --> 00:35:41,961
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't really create... Well, exactly.

471
00:35:41,981 --> 00:35:49,867
[SPEAKER_00]: You only created a particular arrangement of things that were all given beforehand, which means you only... you don't create any substance.

472
00:35:49,887 --> 00:35:53,109
[SPEAKER_00]: You only affect the arrangement of the given substance.

473
00:35:54,310 --> 00:35:59,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are equally on the level of arrangement, not in a substance, your sense of self.

474
00:36:05,750 --> 00:36:10,774
[SPEAKER_00]: But sense of self assumes this ultimate role that not just that it affects arrangement, it controls the substance.

475
00:36:11,094 --> 00:36:12,635
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what sense of self assumes.

476
00:36:12,755 --> 00:36:14,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it comes from the self-sense.

477
00:36:14,657 --> 00:36:15,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

478
00:36:18,239 --> 00:36:19,500
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing that you can create.

479
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:21,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely nothing that you can create.

480
00:36:24,103 --> 00:36:25,504
[SPEAKER_00]: From that fundamental point of view.

481
00:36:27,126 --> 00:36:34,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything you can do... Well, anything that you can do means it's anything that you can intend and attend.

482
00:36:34,887 --> 00:36:41,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything you can intend and attend, has had to be given beforehand, as an option, to intend it or attend it.

483
00:36:45,275 --> 00:36:45,855
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see that.

484
00:36:46,376 --> 00:36:52,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Every, like, myriad of implications, as options, possibilities, things.

485
00:36:54,122 --> 00:36:56,703
[SPEAKER_00]: They're kind of bound with the purpose, bound with the context.

486
00:36:56,984 --> 00:37:02,528
[SPEAKER_00]: But all of that comes from the background, over which you actually have no control.

487
00:37:03,328 --> 00:37:07,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the direction that you cannot go under and kind of hold it up as yours.

488
00:37:09,113 --> 00:37:19,982
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to do so is to ignore it.

489
00:37:20,002 --> 00:37:24,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So the Buddha doesn't create the dhamma?

490
00:37:24,546 --> 00:37:29,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the thing as most universal, most profound, is the dhamma itself, the Buddha said.

491
00:37:31,176 --> 00:37:31,936
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't create this.

492
00:37:32,216 --> 00:37:33,197
[SPEAKER_00]: No Buddha created this.

493
00:37:33,477 --> 00:37:37,139
[SPEAKER_00]: You just find it like an old path that's been overgrown in the forest.

494
00:37:37,419 --> 00:37:37,779
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

495
00:37:37,940 --> 00:37:40,681
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there.

496
00:37:40,701 --> 00:37:41,041
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

497
00:37:41,061 --> 00:37:41,462
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing.

498
00:37:41,502 --> 00:37:42,902
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing you can possibly create.

499
00:37:46,324 --> 00:37:51,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one has the understanding of dhamma, the vision of dhamma.

500
00:37:51,147 --> 00:37:59,452
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having that as your center, not as your center,

501
00:38:03,496 --> 00:38:04,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

502
00:38:04,357 --> 00:38:06,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're composed in the in the balance of the right principle.

503
00:38:07,179 --> 00:38:08,060
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

504
00:38:09,340 --> 00:38:09,741
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

505
00:38:10,681 --> 00:38:18,707
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when sometimes they will insist and ask the Buddha on the... oh, what's the... what happens... what happens for an ārahant?

506
00:38:18,727 --> 00:38:19,928
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens when there is no self?

507
00:38:20,088 --> 00:38:20,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And things like that.

508
00:38:22,030 --> 00:38:27,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he would either just dismiss the questions on self and not-self, if they're formulated wrongly, or the

509
00:38:29,097 --> 00:38:31,819
[SPEAKER_00]: The most direct answer he would give to that would be quite indirect.

510
00:38:32,060 --> 00:38:33,901
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be the description of what teaches him upādāna.

511
00:38:34,181 --> 00:38:35,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what's left.

512
00:38:35,262 --> 00:38:35,983
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that balance.

513
00:38:36,503 --> 00:38:37,344
[SPEAKER_00]: With this, this is.

514
00:38:38,465 --> 00:38:39,866
[SPEAKER_00]: With ignorance, this is.

515
00:38:40,046 --> 00:38:41,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Without ignorance, this is.

516
00:38:41,347 --> 00:38:41,688
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

517
00:38:42,548 --> 00:38:44,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the experience is without ignorance, that's it.

518
00:38:44,650 --> 00:38:45,631
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the arahant's balance.

519
00:38:46,432 --> 00:38:49,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in a way, even Putujana is in a balance.

520
00:38:49,174 --> 00:38:55,219
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, even Putujana, with all his ignorance, can't do anything different to the nature of the five aggregates.

521
00:38:56,634 --> 00:39:00,597
[SPEAKER_00]: The only thing different there is his appropriation of it.

522
00:39:00,917 --> 00:39:07,161
[SPEAKER_00]: The gratuitous appropriation, that's not the aggregates, that's just an attitude in regard to them.

523
00:39:07,341 --> 00:39:11,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely baseless attitude.

524
00:39:11,584 --> 00:39:13,905
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he said Avijay is beginningless.

525
00:39:15,226 --> 00:39:18,588
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no first point when he was, oh, on account of this Avijay came.

526
00:39:18,628 --> 00:39:20,330
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's just there, but it shouldn't be there.

527
00:39:21,010 --> 00:39:25,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you understand that it cannot be there, that's where it disappears.

528
00:39:28,065 --> 00:39:45,115
[SPEAKER_01]: What comes to mind is that the middle way, in the way of right composure, balance, that is, it's not moderation, the way of moderation, the dhamma is not... No, not necessarily, no.

529
00:39:45,995 --> 00:39:50,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when people act out of the view of moderation,

530
00:39:51,338 --> 00:39:59,762
[SPEAKER_00]: It's their self-view that decides what's moderate, based on things that they've experienced, which means moderation, it's a very relative thing.

531
00:39:59,782 --> 00:40:02,443
[SPEAKER_00]: What's moderate for somebody, won't be moderating for somebody else.

532
00:40:03,044 --> 00:40:05,385
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, eventually, it won't be even moderating for you enough.

533
00:40:06,385 --> 00:40:10,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the moderation is only in regard to your previous behavior.

534
00:40:10,207 --> 00:40:14,489
[SPEAKER_00]: But if your behavior was already behavior moderation, then the new moderation is going to be even more moderate.

535
00:40:15,350 --> 00:40:20,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what happened to giants, when they were just going further and further and further in saying those things.

536
00:40:20,722 --> 00:40:24,884
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also when they asked the Buddha... Well, the Buddha asked the man...

537
00:40:24,904 --> 00:40:31,026
[SPEAKER_00]: The man praised the Buddha, and then he asked, what do you think... Why do you think that all these disciples have respect for me?

538
00:40:31,606 --> 00:40:34,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And the man said, ah, because Master Gautama praises eating little and...

539
00:40:34,948 --> 00:40:36,708
[SPEAKER_00]: He eats little and he praises eating little.

540
00:40:37,469 --> 00:40:40,350
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives rough, he plays living rough, and so on.

541
00:40:40,370 --> 00:40:44,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, well, if they were true, means that I have...

542
00:40:46,442 --> 00:40:49,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes I eat a bowl full, full to the brim.

543
00:40:49,643 --> 00:40:52,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I have a second one, full to the brim.

544
00:40:52,824 --> 00:40:58,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have disciples that, at the same time, would live on a... on house rice, just a handful every two or three days would get.

545
00:40:58,646 --> 00:40:59,026
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

546
00:40:59,106 --> 00:40:59,807
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all they would eat.

547
00:41:00,427 --> 00:41:04,908
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were to respect me on account of eating little, they wouldn't, because they eat less than me.

548
00:41:06,189 --> 00:41:08,890
[SPEAKER_00]: And it goes on about any kind of ascetic practice.

549
00:41:09,470 --> 00:41:14,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes I stay in king's palaces, while I have disciples living under a root of tree.

550
00:41:14,331 --> 00:41:16,152
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were to respect me on account of that, they wouldn't.

551
00:41:17,585 --> 00:41:18,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do they respect me?

552
00:41:18,526 --> 00:41:20,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I taught suffering and freedom from it.

553
00:41:20,467 --> 00:41:22,989
[SPEAKER_00]: I taught pratīcṭhāna, upādāna, and he lists all these different ways.

554
00:41:23,029 --> 00:41:26,832
[SPEAKER_00]: I taught jhānas, all these different ways of practice towards understanding.

555
00:41:27,672 --> 00:41:29,133
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, like, that's why they respect me.

556
00:41:29,173 --> 00:41:37,479
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of that moderation or asceticism, if it's taken further, sometimes helpful for somebody, sometimes it isn't.

557
00:41:37,639 --> 00:41:40,101
[SPEAKER_00]: But the criteria is not what I think helpful is.

558
00:41:40,141 --> 00:41:43,323
[SPEAKER_00]: The criteria is that vision of dhamma that you open up with.

559
