740
00:55:54,325 --> 00:56:11,209
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any subha-nimitta, if you discern long enough, you realize, ah, whenever there is... You can have a subha that's a subha, or you can have a subha that's less a subha, which is how it's perceived to be from the sensuality point of view, which is subha then.

741
00:56:11,349 --> 00:56:17,170
[SPEAKER_00]: The sign of beauty, it's basically the sign of less ugly, but still pertaining to the nature of ugly.

742
00:56:18,771 --> 00:56:19,931
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like beautiful and ugly.

743
00:56:19,991 --> 00:56:20,931
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ugly and less ugly.

744
00:56:22,766 --> 00:56:25,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Disgusting and slightly less disgusting, but equally disgusting.

745
00:56:26,668 --> 00:56:33,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, a spoonful of excrement is less disgusting than a bucketful, if you have to, you know, engage with it.

746
00:56:34,691 --> 00:56:36,571
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's actually equally disgusting.

747
00:56:38,292 --> 00:56:45,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But people forget that great... that greater context of sharing the nature of disgusting, and then, oh no, this is actually quite agreeable.

748
00:56:47,055 --> 00:56:51,136
[SPEAKER_00]: The less disgusting becomes desirable, becomes your sensuality.

749
00:56:51,676 --> 00:56:56,377
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's equally disgusting, in terms of pertaining to the nature of it.

750
00:56:56,857 --> 00:57:00,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Just less disgusting in its content, but the nature is equally disgusting.

751
00:57:02,939 --> 00:57:08,700
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can only see that once, first, by accepting the sublimity for what it is.

752
00:57:08,720 --> 00:57:13,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only accept it by stop acting out of it, stop indulging it, stop being non-restrained and so on.

753
00:57:14,081 --> 00:57:16,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when you accept it, then you get to see it, and then you can see further.

754
00:57:18,288 --> 00:57:25,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can see that there is a subha-nimitta and a subha-nimitta, or there can be subha-nimitta, which means that's a subha-nimitta there as well.

755
00:57:27,054 --> 00:57:29,516
[SPEAKER_00]: A subha can be present through subha and a subha.

756
00:57:34,059 --> 00:57:37,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it doesn't matter if it's subha or a subha, a subha is there.

757
00:57:38,523 --> 00:57:47,369
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter if it's unattractive, disgusting, or a little bit less unattractive and a little bit less disgusting, the disgust is there.

758
00:57:53,009 --> 00:57:57,870
[SPEAKER_01]: The foreground is diversified.

759
00:57:58,870 --> 00:58:00,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the foreground diversifies, sure.

760
00:58:00,611 --> 00:58:03,571
[SPEAKER_01]: The background is more... unifies.

761
00:58:04,511 --> 00:58:05,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

762
00:58:05,832 --> 00:58:11,473
[SPEAKER_00]: The background is that... it's where the nature of that... it's that which determines the diversity.

763
00:58:12,173 --> 00:58:19,354
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a reference point for diversity, a reference point for a change, a reference point for time-lapsing.

764
00:58:20,395 --> 00:58:22,475
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that, it's pertaining to the foreground.

765
00:58:24,303 --> 00:58:33,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something needs to remain unchanged while change occurs, so that you can see that there is a change.

766
00:58:34,849 --> 00:58:43,054
[SPEAKER_00]: If everything is changing all the time, constantly, well, there is no change at all, because you can't tell that everything is changing, because everything is changing.

767
00:58:46,135 --> 00:58:52,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Or things can change at different rate, perhaps, and then the thing that changes at a lesser rate,

768
00:58:53,322 --> 00:58:56,143
[SPEAKER_00]: It can become a reference point for a thing that changes at a quicker rate.

769
00:58:58,543 --> 00:59:00,924
[SPEAKER_00]: But that might be too abstract for some people to ponder on.

770
00:59:01,544 --> 00:59:08,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can, you can, you can, you can practice that on the level of feelings and moods, like the general feelings.

771
00:59:10,046 --> 00:59:15,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you can have a general, a general feeling present of you being okay or you being not okay.

772
00:59:17,248 --> 00:59:18,888
[SPEAKER_00]: While they're particular,

773
00:59:21,442 --> 00:59:27,003
[SPEAKER_00]: More diversified feelings occurring on a daily basis.

774
00:59:27,043 --> 00:59:38,446
[SPEAKER_00]: But overall, you're okay, you're meeting in all sorts of disagreeable feelings on an individual level, like, oh, this is annoying, this is disappointing and stuff, but none of that to the point of changing your general feeling.

775
00:59:39,726 --> 00:59:48,788
[SPEAKER_00]: But when that general feeling changes, and it will, then even the other things that you're encountering that are agreeable,

776
00:59:49,842 --> 00:59:53,405
[SPEAKER_00]: will not necessarily make you happy or bring you back into the good mood.

777
00:59:54,705 --> 01:00:01,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could see there, like, it becomes almost like a reference point, the general feeling to the particular feelings that you have.

778
01:00:02,871 --> 01:00:07,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Reference point that determines whether you're happy or not, really, whether you're suffering or not.

779
01:00:08,335 --> 01:00:13,739
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when people are in a good mood and they can take on the world, because nothing can make them suffer, because this mood is there.

780
01:00:14,419 --> 01:00:18,382
[SPEAKER_00]: But they fail to see that that mood has arisen on its own, which means, as such, it will have to go on its own.

781
01:00:19,654 --> 01:00:26,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means then, if you established your whole well-being upon that mood, when that mood goes, you're going to be compromised, seriously compromised.

782
01:00:27,979 --> 01:00:39,547
[SPEAKER_00]: But the more you establish the mind on the background... Well, you want to be starting to discern peripheral in regard to the peripheral moods.

783
01:00:40,707 --> 01:00:41,188
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

784
01:00:41,228 --> 01:00:41,988
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the principle.

785
01:00:42,548 --> 01:00:44,830
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not always, like, from that point of view,

786
01:00:45,658 --> 01:00:54,345
[SPEAKER_00]: The new peripheral, in regard to general moods, would be that, almost like equanimity, would be the sign of the nimitta, in regard to the general moods.

787
01:00:56,727 --> 01:01:00,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just see this feeling comes and goes, this feeling comes and goes, this feeling comes and goes.

788
01:01:01,371 --> 01:01:03,232
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be in common to these feelings?

789
01:01:03,332 --> 01:01:04,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not coming and going.

790
01:01:05,494 --> 01:01:07,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Just sort of being there.

791
01:01:07,295 --> 01:01:08,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Phenomenon that's not being felt.

792
01:01:09,737 --> 01:01:11,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like nibbāna is the ultimate dhamma.

793
01:01:12,602 --> 01:01:15,223
[SPEAKER_00]: As in the most general fundamental dhamma.

794
01:01:15,263 --> 01:01:16,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still a dhamma.

795
01:01:17,024 --> 01:01:20,986
[SPEAKER_00]: It's nothing transcendental in a sense of outside of this experience.

796
01:01:21,046 --> 01:01:25,188
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still a dhamma, but a dhamma that's free from greed, aversion, delusion, free from being felt.

797
01:01:26,049 --> 01:01:30,851
[SPEAKER_01]: That now has became pretty much the mode of your existence here and now.

798
01:01:32,592 --> 01:01:38,415
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind has been developed to such an apex that is just unshakable from that point of view.

799
01:01:38,435 --> 01:01:39,135
[SPEAKER_00]: It's irreversible.

800
01:01:41,427 --> 01:01:43,688
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the balance, the balance, the perfected balance.

801
01:01:45,229 --> 01:01:48,411
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's just the notion of imbalance disappears, it's inconceivable.

802
01:01:51,413 --> 01:02:10,844
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, if you look at the phenomenon of things, some things in general, things, experience, whatever is in front of you, the most general experience, and then what is

803
01:02:12,220 --> 01:02:13,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimately speaking, yes.

804
01:02:13,621 --> 01:02:15,822
[SPEAKER_01]: The ultimate negative, the nothingness.

805
01:02:15,842 --> 01:02:16,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

806
01:02:16,362 --> 01:02:18,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can go even further.

807
01:02:18,563 --> 01:02:20,684
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say whatever is the foreground.

808
01:02:20,704 --> 01:02:22,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever, anything that arises,

809
01:02:42,578 --> 01:02:48,622
[SPEAKER_00]: What do all those things have in common?

810
01:02:48,642 --> 01:02:49,362
[SPEAKER_00]: That they're present.

811
01:02:50,363 --> 01:02:53,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, then, the ultimate sign to that is absence.

812
01:02:54,866 --> 01:02:56,066
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility of absence.

813
01:02:56,727 --> 01:02:57,888
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's nothing other than death.

814
01:02:58,228 --> 01:02:59,729
[SPEAKER_00]: The notion of death that people have.

815
01:03:01,070 --> 01:03:04,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why the Buddha did say, the one who practices mindfulness of death, it's like the quickest way.

816
01:03:06,133 --> 01:03:10,035
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nibbāna, it's almost like dying without actually dying.

817
01:03:11,839 --> 01:03:19,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Extinguishing your being, extinguishing your sense of self, extinguishing your likes, your affections, your attractions.

818
01:03:20,162 --> 01:03:21,562
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people are terrified from.

819
01:03:22,722 --> 01:03:34,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Even back in the day, when the Buddha would lay it out for them, and they would get shocked, panic, fall down on the ground in despair, pulling their hair, shouting that, well, if we practice that, that will kill me.

820
01:03:34,866 --> 01:03:36,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's exactly what dhamma is.

821
01:03:37,487 --> 01:03:38,227
[SPEAKER_00]: For Putujana,

822
01:03:38,920 --> 01:03:45,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma is if they go through the notion of their own death without dying, without doing anything on account of it.

823
01:03:47,286 --> 01:04:00,215
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why many practitioners turn away from it and jump onto the rituals, rules, traditions, whatever else, so they don't take responsibility, because if they do, it becomes apparent that it's on you and means if you keep doing it, you will die.

824
01:04:00,636 --> 01:04:02,037
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what even Ajahn Chah said.

825
01:04:03,077 --> 01:04:05,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Our practice of dhamma is like die, you should die.

826
01:04:05,979 --> 01:04:08,181
[SPEAKER_00]: If you practice dhamma, you will die before you die.

827
01:04:09,468 --> 01:04:14,291
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just a poetic expression.

828
01:04:14,332 --> 01:04:16,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like, you know, edgy inspiration.

829
01:04:16,473 --> 01:04:17,113
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's a fact.

830
01:04:17,514 --> 01:04:21,556
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people... Those people understood what nibbāna was.

831
01:04:21,576 --> 01:04:25,439
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the thing, when the Buddha would teach them.

832
01:04:25,459 --> 01:04:31,223
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha would say what his teaching is, they would understand what he was talking for nibbāna to be.

833
01:04:31,883 --> 01:04:32,824
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would freak out.

834
01:04:34,585 --> 01:04:37,107
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... And again, it's nothing.

835
01:04:37,147 --> 01:04:38,568
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing transcendental in it.

836
01:04:39,285 --> 01:04:43,246
[SPEAKER_00]: People are not in nibbāna is because they don't value the nothingness.

837
01:04:43,686 --> 01:04:46,627
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't value the absence of greed, aversion, delusion.

838
01:04:46,647 --> 01:04:51,809
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't value the imperturbable, not being felt, unshakable states.

839
01:04:51,889 --> 01:04:52,789
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't value that.

840
01:04:53,929 --> 01:04:55,450
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you know that they don't value?

841
01:04:55,550 --> 01:05:05,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, just have somebody, yourself, if you have doubts, lock yourself in a room, don't have any distraction, don't do anything for a whole day, just one day, and see.

842
01:05:06,479 --> 01:05:08,960
[SPEAKER_00]: How close to the freaking out you're going to get with boredom.

843
01:05:09,021 --> 01:05:12,382
[SPEAKER_00]: First boredom, then it's going to turn into dread and anxiety.

844
01:05:12,422 --> 01:05:13,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly it.

845
01:05:13,703 --> 01:05:15,584
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that peaceful feeling of nibbāna.

846
01:05:16,465 --> 01:05:19,726
[SPEAKER_00]: But Pratyujana experienced it as a dread and anxiety.

847
01:05:19,766 --> 01:05:20,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Not me, not my control.

848
01:05:20,747 --> 01:05:22,048
[SPEAKER_00]: I better distract myself from this.

849
01:05:23,128 --> 01:05:27,831
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he were to prepare himself to not be afraid of that feeling, that's that peace.

850
01:05:27,871 --> 01:05:35,795
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Buddha would say that the neutral feeling, the sublime neutral feeling, the refined neutral feeling,

851
01:05:36,145 --> 01:05:38,245
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, it's valued by the wise.

852
01:05:39,506 --> 01:05:42,886
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pleasant when it's known, and unpleasant when it isn't known.

853
01:05:44,487 --> 01:05:45,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Valuing it, knowing it.

854
01:05:45,647 --> 01:05:46,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

855
01:05:46,727 --> 01:05:48,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, they can value it because they know it.

856
01:05:48,447 --> 01:05:50,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, no, this is actually the true peace.

857
01:05:51,268 --> 01:05:58,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Putujana doesn't have peace, doesn't have nibbāna, not because his eyes are covered by the metaphysical veil of māyā and illusion.

858
01:05:59,110 --> 01:06:00,910
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's because he just doesn't value it.

859
01:06:02,378 --> 01:06:06,199
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't recognize it first, then he doesn't value it, even if he were to recognize it.

860
01:06:07,240 --> 01:06:09,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Even further, he's actively afraid of it.

861
01:06:11,141 --> 01:06:12,401
[SPEAKER_00]: He's terrified of it.

862
01:06:15,522 --> 01:06:16,783
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he's not in nibbāna.

863
01:06:18,883 --> 01:06:23,545
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to stop being afraid of your own death, for example, and you're already closer to nibbāna.

864
01:06:28,842 --> 01:06:30,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's why people are afraid of death.

865
01:06:30,823 --> 01:06:38,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because they know what death is, but because death is just this vague direction of absence of all of this, that I hold dear.

866
01:06:38,566 --> 01:06:38,926
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

867
01:06:40,026 --> 01:06:45,588
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be afraid of death, because you don't... you can't even see the death coming.

868
01:06:45,628 --> 01:06:47,509
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always in the direction external to you.

869
01:06:48,289 --> 01:06:50,430
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always from the opposing direction from where you look.

870
01:06:51,504 --> 01:07:01,909
[SPEAKER_00]: You being afraid of death, it's only possible because you see a relationship, you see a reference to absence and denial of everything that you hold dear in your life.

871
01:07:02,909 --> 01:07:08,912
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, because you're attached to life, you fear the absence of life, which is what death is.

872
01:07:09,052 --> 01:07:18,496
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove the attachment to life, the kind of attitude of holding it dear and delighting in it, why would you be afraid if the whole thing goes?

873
01:07:18,856 --> 01:07:20,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear will become inconceivable.

874
01:07:21,141 --> 01:07:26,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why arahant is ultimately beyond death, beyond that fear of that relationship of absence.

875
01:07:28,647 --> 01:07:35,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Because puthujjana can't even conceive death.

876
01:07:35,532 --> 01:07:37,793
[SPEAKER_00]: When you think death, you think in positive terms again.

877
01:07:40,576 --> 01:07:48,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for many people, just a mere change, it's almost on the level of freaking out as if they would when they experience death.

878
01:07:48,762 --> 01:07:50,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone if things actually do start.

879
01:07:52,059 --> 01:07:55,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Going into the absent mode, so to speak.

880
01:07:55,160 --> 01:07:56,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Losing your senses and so on.

881
01:07:59,262 --> 01:08:00,842
[SPEAKER_00]: But that is the ultimate nimitta.

882
01:08:03,724 --> 01:08:07,445
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you can use those perceptions to establish your mind.

883
01:08:07,865 --> 01:08:09,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Establish your mind from the perception of death.

884
01:08:16,008 --> 01:08:17,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Going beyond perception and feeling.

885
01:08:18,309 --> 01:08:19,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, it would go.

886
01:08:19,630 --> 01:08:20,350
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the sign?

887
01:08:21,799 --> 01:08:22,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence.

888
01:08:22,860 --> 01:08:23,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothingness.

889
01:08:26,564 --> 01:08:34,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will be the ultimate non-indicative, non-appropriating consciousness.

890
01:08:34,531 --> 01:08:37,834
[SPEAKER_00]: As in presence that doesn't appropriate any presence.

891
01:08:40,296 --> 01:08:41,638
[SPEAKER_00]: That will be your experience as a whole.

892
01:08:42,198 --> 01:08:45,261
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like when consciousness doesn't indicate any ownership, any sense of self.

893
01:08:45,923 --> 01:08:49,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Nor the matter finds any footing in that experience anymore.

894
01:08:49,166 --> 01:08:49,707
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just left.

895
01:08:50,007 --> 01:08:51,248
[SPEAKER_00]: In seen, there will be the seen.

896
01:08:51,308 --> 01:08:52,830
[SPEAKER_00]: In heard, just the heard.

897
01:08:53,690 --> 01:08:54,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on for all the senses.

898
01:08:54,871 --> 01:08:55,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

